<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-5-5-modular-division" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Modular Division</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In the previous section, we discussed addition, subtraction, and multiplication modulo <m>m</m>. Here, we discuss when it is possible to divide modulo <m>m</m>.
    </p>
  
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-what-is-division">
    <title>What is Division?</title>

    <p>
      How do we formally define division in standard arithmetic? We do it through multiplication. Note:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        \frac{a}{b} = \frac{1}{b} \cdot a = b^{-1}a.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      What, then, is <m>b^{-1}</m>? It is the unique solution to the equation:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
        bx = 1.
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      We define division in modular arithmetic the same way. In general, to <q>divide</q> by <m>a</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li><p> Solve <m>ax \equiv 1</m> (mod <m>m</m>) to get <m>a^{-1}</m>. </p></li>
        <li><p> Multiple by <m>a^{-1}</m>. </p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-mod-inverse">
    <title>Modular Inverses</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-mod-div">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>a \in \mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>m \in \mathbb{N}</m>. Then:
        </p>

        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>There exists some <m>b \in \mathbb{Z}</m> such that <m>ab \equiv 1</m> (mod <m>m</m>) if and only if <m>\mathrm{gcd}(a, m) = 1</m>.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>If <m>ab_1 \equiv ab_2 \equiv 1</m> (mod <m>m</m>), then <m>b_1 \equiv b_2</m> (mod <m>m</m>).</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>

        <p>
          If such a number <m>b</m> exists, then we write <m>b = a^{-1}</m> and call it the inverse of <m>a</m> modulo <m>m</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <proof>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <!-- Proof of part 1 -->
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>(\impliedby)</m> Suppose <m>\mathrm{gcd}(a, m) = 1</m>. Then, by <xref ref="cor-extended-gcd"/>, there exists integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> such that <m>ax + my = 1</m>. Therefore, <m>ax = 1 - my \equiv 1</m> (mod <m>m</m>). Letting <m>b = x</m> completes the proof.
              </p>
              <p>
                <m>(\implies)</m> Assume <m>ab \equiv 1</m> (mod <m>m</m>). Then there exists an integer <m>k</m> such that <m>ab = 1 - km</m>, which implies <m>ab + km = 1</m>. We know from the proof of <xref ref="thm-gcd-exists"/> that <m>\mathrm{gcd}(a, m) \mid (ab + km)</m> and therefore <m>\mathrm{gcd}(a, m) \mid 1</m>. Hence, <m>\mathrm{gcd}(a, m) = 1</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <!-- Proof of part 2 -->
            <li>
              <p>
                Notice:
              </p>

              <p>
                <md>
                  b_1 \equiv b_1 \cdot 1 \equiv b_1 \cdot (ab_2) \equiv (ab_1) \cdot b_2 \equiv 1 \cdot b_2 \equiv b_2.
                </md>
              </p>

              <p>
                Hence, <m>b_1 \equiv b_2</m> (mod <m>m</m>).
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>
    
    <example xml:id="ex-div-by-2">
      <title>
        Division by <m>2</m> modulo <m>5</m>
      </title>
    
      <statement>
        <p>
          We know that <m>\mathrm{gcd}(2, 5) = 1</m>, so <m>2^{-1}</m> exists modulo <m>5</m>. What is it? 
        </p>
      </statement>
      
      <solution>
        <p>
          One way to do this is to guess and check.
        </p>

        <p>
          <md number="no">
            <mrow> 2 (1) \amp\equiv 2, </mrow>
            <mrow> 2 (2) \amp\equiv 4, </mrow>
            <mrow> 2 (3) \amp\equiv 1. </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          Hence, <m>2^{-1} = 3</m> (mod <m>5</m>).
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      The strategy used in <xref ref="ex-div-by-2"/> works for small numbers, but if we're working with <m>m \approx 10^{100}</m>, it is not feasible. In those cases, we can use the Euclidean algorithm!
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-div-by-17">
        <title>
          Division by <m>17</m> modulo <m>74</m>
        </title>
      
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the Euclidean algorithm to find <m>17^{-1}</m> modulo <m>74</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        
        <solution>
          <p>
            <em>Step 1</em>: Run the Euclidean algorithm:
          </p>
          
          <p>
            <md number="no">
              <mrow> 74 \amp = 17 \cdot 4 + 6 </mrow>
              <mrow> 17 \amp = 6  \cdot 2 + 5 </mrow>
              <mrow>  6 \amp = 5  \cdot 1 + 1 </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>

          <p>
            <em>Step 2</em>: Solve each equation above for the remainders, starting from the top. Let <m>a = 74</m> and <m>b = 17</m>. Then:
          </p>

          <p>
            <md alignment="alignat" number="no">
              <mrow> 6 \amp = 74 - 17 \cdot 4 \amp\amp = a - 4b.                      \amp\amp             </mrow>
              <mrow> 5 \amp = 17 - 6  \cdot 2 \amp\amp = b - (a - 4b) \cdot 2         \amp\amp = 9b - 2a.  </mrow>
              <mrow> 1 \amp =  6 - 5  \cdot 1 \amp\amp = (a - 4b) - (9b - 2a) \cdot 1 \amp\amp = 3a - 13b. </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>

          <p>
            <em>Step 3</em>: Reduce modulo <m>a = 74</m>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <md>
              3a - 13b = 1 \implies -13b \equiv 1 \, (\text{mod } 74) \implies 61b \equiv 1 \, (\text{mod } 74),
            </md>
          </p>

          <p>
            Since <m>-13 \equiv 61</m> (mod <m>74</m>). Thus, <m>17^{-1} = 63</m> modulo <m>74</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
    </example> 

    <p>
      The process outlined in <xref ref="ex-div-by-17"/> is referred to as the <em>extended Euclidean algorithm</em> and is the practical standard for computing modular inverses for moduli <m>m \lesssim 2^{2048}</m>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>